          "Two consequences before you write policy. First, for your participation: the intellectualist analysis does not make the target innocent — wrongs are real, correction is owed; it makes your anger's premise false and your pile-on contribution what it is: retribution's smallest unit, dosed by no one, aimed at a person whose error called for the physician of 706 and received ten thousand avengers instead. Ask the only Stoic question: what does my contribution correct? If the answer is nothing — and arithmetic says the ten-thousand-and-first quote-post corrects nothing — then the post is expressive, and what it expresses, per 706, is a false belief about a stranger's mind, published for the metric. Second, for your reception: premeditate the other direction — the day the fragment is yours. The pile-on received is the opinion-of-others doctrine at examination difficulty: thousands of strangers, wrong about your mind, in public, simultaneously. Prepare the analysis now, while it is cheap: their judgments are events in their prohairesis, built from a fragment you did not control, by the mechanism you have just studied; the number of the wrong does not convert opinion into fact; and your response — correction, apology where owed, silence where not — is the one component that is up to you.",
