          "Epictetus's 3.12 then disciplines the practice against its two corruptions, and both stalk the digital fast specifically. First corruption: training as display. His scorn is for the ascetic performing austerity — 'if you have determined to train yourself to endurance, do it for yourself and not for those outside... embrace the statue in winter where none can see' (the reference is to Diogenes' famous public winter-statue-embracing, which Epictetus pointedly privatizes). The digital translation is almost too easy: the detox announced on the platform being detoxed from — 'taking a break, back in a week' with its harvest of supportive comments — is the fast converted to content, the abstention performing for the exact metric-audience whose grip the abstention was meant to break; Session 3's machinery, wearing sackcloth. The rule: the fast is unannounced except to those whose coordination it practically requires (the partner, the colleague expecting replies), and its results live in your log, not your feed. Second corruption: training as ostentation toward oneself — difficulty for difficulty's sake, the spiritual athlete collecting austerities. 3.12's standard: training is 'for the sake of desire and aversion, that you may not fail to get what you desire nor fall into what you avoid' — always instrumental, always aimed at a named weakness of assent, never an end. The fast that has stopped producing data and started producing identity ('I am someone who doesn't...') has quietly become a possession, and possessions, per Session 5, get audited.",
